,\n  1406→      \"flag_reason\": \"homograph\",\n  1407→      \"occurrences\": 1,\n  1408→      \"first_position\": 11611,\n  1409→      \"chapter_indices\": [\n  1410→        1\n  1411→      ],\n  1412→      \"context_examples\": [\n  1413→        \"...his group in the direction of the intruder, who, at the moment was also near at hand, and now, with deliberate and stately step, made closer approach to the speaker. But from a certain nameless awe with which t...\"\n  1414→      ],\n  1415→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  1416→      \"ipa\": null,\n  1417→      \"phonetic_spelling\": null,\n  1418→      \"audio_url\": null,\n  1419→      \"user_pronunciation\": null,\n  1420→      \"notes\": \"Multiple pronunciations: adj (di-LIB-er-it); verb (di-LIB-er-ayt)\"\n  1421→    }\n  1422→  ],\n  1423→  \"glossary\": null,\n  1424→  \"overview\": {\n  1425→    \"structure\": {\n  1426→      \"chapter_count\": 1,\n  1427→      \"has_titles\": false,\n  1428→      \"has_parts\": false,\n  1429→      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n  1430→      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n  1431→    },\n  1432→    \"plot_summary\": {\n  1433→      \"plot_summary\": \"The story begins in the midst of a devastating plague known as the Red Death, a merciless disease marked by bloody pores and swift, fatal onset. As the world outside crumbles into chaos and death, Prince Prospero, a man of extravagant tastes and self-imposed authority, retreats with a thousand courtiers into the secluded, fortified abbey he has transformed into a sanctuary of luxury and excess. The abbey, a labyrinth of seven uniquely decorated rooms—each adorned with stained-glass windows matching their color schemes—serves as both refuge and prison, its isolation a testament to Prospero’s belief in his own invincibility. The central chamber, however, is a chilling anomaly: a black room with blood-red panes, a silent omen of the doom that will soon claim them all. The night unfolds with a grand masked ball, a grotesque celebration of life in the face of death, illuminated only by flickering firelight from tripods that cast long, dancing shadows across the walls.\\n\\nAs the revelry reaches its peak, a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, its form drenched in blood and masked with a corpse-like visage. The figure moves silently through the seven rooms, inciting terror and paralysis among the guests, who are too intoxicated by their own illusions of safety to recognize the horror they face. Prince Prospero, enraged by the intrusion and the disruption of his carefully curated world, takes up a dagger and pursues the figure through each chamber, his pride and sense of control unraveling with every step. The chase becomes a symbolic descent into the truth he has long denied: that no fortress, no wealth, no isolation can shield one from fate. The climax occurs in the black chamber, where the figure is revealed to be empty—no man, no disguise, only the embodiment of the plague itself.\\n\\nIn that moment, Prospero collapses dead, his arrogance undone by the realization that the Red Death was never an outsider but a presence he had invited through his denial of mortality. The plague then spreads unchecked through the abbey, claiming every guest in a final, silent surrender. The great clock that had marked the hours of revelry stops, the lights extinguish, and darkness consumes the once-gleaming sanctuary. The story ends not with redemption or escape, but with the absolute triumph of death over illusion, a chilling reminder that no man, no matter how powerful or isolated, can outrun his fate. The narrative arc moves from denial and indulgence to confrontation and inevitable collapse, underscoring the futility of human attempts to transcend natural law.\",\n  1434→      \"themes\": [\n  1435→        \"identity\",\n  1436→        \"ambition\",\n  1437→        \"loss\"\n  1438→      ],\n  1439→      \"narrative_sty